course: dictionary
lesson: 7850
title: Radioactive: energy from breaking atoms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A radioactive material gives off energy that you cannot see, called radiation. The energy comes from inside its atoms, because their centres are slowly breaking apart. «The inside is hot because of heat left from when the Earth formed and because radioactive elements in the rock slowly break down and give off heat.» The clues are «break down» and «give off heat», so radioactive means giving off energy as atoms break apart.
explain: Be careful: heat alone does not make something radioactive. «Microwave ovens heat food by making its water molecules jiggle; this does not make food radioactive.» Jiggling water makes food hot, but the atoms are not breaking apart, so the food is not radioactive. Always check the clues around the word.
explain: Radioactive also has a joking meaning. Friends might say a very unpopular song is radioactive, meaning everyone runs away from it. Try swapping your meaning into the sentence: if «radioactive» can be replaced by «so unpopular that everybody avoids it» and it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Radioactive means giving off strong, invisible energy called radiation.
fact: Radiation comes from the centres of atoms breaking apart.
fact: Radioactive materials are used in hospitals to treat cancer and to take scans.
fact: Too much radiation damages living cells, so «radioactive material» must be handled with great care.
fact: The warning sign for radioactivity is a yellow-and-black trefoil, a shape like three blades around a circle.
exercise: «The inside is hot because radioactive elements in the rock slowly break down and give off heat.» What does radioactive mean here? | Shiny and smooth | Cold and damp | Made of water | *Giving off energy as atoms break apart
exercise: «Microwave ovens heat food by making its water molecules jiggle; this does not make food radioactive.» Why is food not radioactive after a microwave? | *Jiggling water makes food hot, but no atoms are breaking apart | Hot food always gives off radiation | Microwaves are radioactive, so food is too | Water molecules are radioactive
exercise: The yellow-and-black trefoil is a warning sign. What does it warn you about? | A bicycle lane | *Radioactive danger | Free sweets ahead
test: «Marie Curie discovered the radioactive elements polonium and radium.» What does radioactive tell you about these elements? | They are cold and easy to touch | *They give off radiation because their atoms are breaking apart | They glow only in sunlight | They are made of water
test: A friend says the school dinner is totally radioactive, so nobody sits near it. What does radioactive mean in this joke? | Made of metal | *So unpopular and off-putting that everyone stays away | Glowing in the dark | Very hot to touch
exam: Inside a radioactive material, what is slowly breaking apart? | The warning sign | *The centres of its atoms | Radiation | Water molecules
